WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  inside sri lanka's most heavily guarded  building its new president was chosen  mps cast secret ballots to decide who  would succeed gotabe rajapaksa  he fled in a military plane last week  after tens of thousands of protesters  stormed his official residence  the prospect of ranil vikramasingha  succeeding rajapaksa had caused an  outcry from thousands who were demanding  that he too stepped down  but more than half the parliamentarians  ignored the protesters wishes  the time of being divided is over we had  48 hours of division now i'm asking  everyone to get together to discuss i'd  like to start discussions with all of  you from tomorrow  [Music]  many sri lankans are angry that a man  voted out of parliament in the last  election has become president he is the  defender of the rajapaksa family the  second reason is he doesn't have a  mandate to be a member of parliament at  least so we do not accept his  appointment  public opinion is opposed to this we the  trade unions are opposed to it the  people are opposed to it we are clearly  opposed to raniel's appointment this is  not the people's will it's a distortion  one of the first things wikrama singh  had did was thank the armed forces and  police  hundreds of security personnel secured  the parliament complex to ensure  wednesday's vote was not disrupted  analysts say his immediate challenge  will be to pull sri lanka back from the  brink of total disaster  a moderate parliamentarian in the  rajapaksa's slpp party told me the  election was a battle between money and  conscience protesters say given their  elected representatives did not reflect  their views when choosing their  candidate  they will continue to oppose the  presidency of runner vikrama singha  fernandez al jazeera colombo

